  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "The length of a square is increased by 3 and the width is decreased by 3. The resulting rectangle has three quarters the area of the square. Find the side length of the original square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$6$",
      "worked": "Let the original side length be $x$. The square has area $x^2$ and the rectangle has area $(x-3)(x+3)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(x-3)(x+3)&=\\frac34x^2\\\\x^2-9&=\\frac34x^2\\\\4x^2-36&=3x^2\\\\x^2&=36\\\\x&=6\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe original side length is $6$, taking the positive root because it is a length.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
